#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151910 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "88fe37cf-5df2-5988-90b8-082f7aaca609" :question/math-academy-id "e-6791" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + y = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 2, \\quad y'(\\pi) = -1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A \\cos x + B \\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find constants $A$ and $B.$ To do this, we apply the initial conditions $y(\\pi)=2,$ $y'(\\pi)=-1.$\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =2 \\\\ A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  & =2 \\\\ -A & =2 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $y'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\ny' =  - A \\sin{x} + B\\cos{x},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(\\pi)=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-1 \\\\ -A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & =-1 \\\\ -B & =-1 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -2\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "4739ced1-bbcc-516b-971f-a580ec97961a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6861" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0,\n\\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2}=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "95cdd4cc-df70-5dac-8bd3-66edb012cdcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6862" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 4 y' + 4y =0,\n\\qquad y(0) =  3, \\qquad y'(0) = 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda = 2,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny'  = 2 A e ^ {2 x} +  B e ^ { 2 x} + 2 B x e ^{ 2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 2 & =2A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=A \\\\ 2=2A+B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =6+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4,$ and therefore the solution is \n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ { 2 x } - 4 x e ^ { 2 x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151935 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "80968044-25e2-5906-929c-b944f84ca27d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7051" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2,  \\quad y'(0) = 5, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2 = P.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+{e}^{3x}(-4P\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3{e}^{0}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+{e}^{0}(-4(2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\ 5 & =6+4Q \\\\ -1 & =4Q \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( 2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315616c0-7ca3-580e-b2b9-f7fd7049408a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{e}^{10x}+x{e}^{10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348769} {:answer/id #uuid "13dee64f-f6bb-5e99-b7b9-f7b28103d9e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{-10x}+x{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348770} {:answer/id #uuid "072e8115-4c1b-5616-a167-9e243dc4db80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{-10x}+45x{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348771} {:answer/id #uuid "7c19de91-0967-58a8-a35f-f9e5c6cace4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{10x}+2{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348772} {:answer/id #uuid "b87f57eb-57b8-5883-8c46-1474293ae08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{-10x}+27x{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072e8115-4c1b-5616-a167-9e243dc4db80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{-10x}+45x{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348771} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bbd7185-494e-5b1c-8d62-575dd8301a37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe25c50d-bb40-5711-844b-9e4bb7250580" :question/math-academy-id "q-130345" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+20{y}^{′}+100y=0,\\,y(0)=5,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+20\\lambda +100 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +10)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda =-10,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-10x}+Bx{e}^{-10x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=5.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-10A{e}^{-10x}+B{e}^{-10x}-10Bx{e}^{-10x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-5$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-10A+B=-5.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=5$ gives $B=45.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{-10x}+45x{e}^{-10x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c576f38-4fa5-5359-9617-f3c7f962e765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}-2x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348776} {:answer/id #uuid "3b680f27-1a8b-5f6c-acdb-7a04cbdb139d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}-x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348777} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e72572-e67b-5175-8cb9-b67bd5badd70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348778} {:answer/id #uuid "a463efeb-a4db-53c2-aaf4-0b3faac4f89f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}-3x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348779} {:answer/id #uuid "b3149b42-287a-5dcd-91ec-15d80ef84994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3149b42-287a-5dcd-91ec-15d80ef84994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348780} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0e44e5d-3fba-52e8-ac06-b67831bff6eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21de8ce6-7686-5e0d-ab4d-25c83ba08786" :question/math-academy-id "q-130348" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-4Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-4A+B=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=1$ gives $B=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d2e44a5-505c-53cf-bc55-fb16db32e187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348783} {:answer/id #uuid "9def854b-cdb9-5a8d-85f1-ddc7bb39fe33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348784} {:answer/id #uuid "f0835121-0568-56a0-8b34-e714e0a83785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348785} {:answer/id #uuid "c7da6a7a-9196-5b48-bd9f-126e43724726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348786} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2428b2-73a5-5b19-81f2-ae9abd4c30b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7da6a7a-9196-5b48-bd9f-126e43724726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348786} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe1d37c-447f-5c6f-9fba-10123ca1e96d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1c172fd-ed64-5bde-930c-1a084c8ba535" :question/math-academy-id "q-130355" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+100y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=10,\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=50.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 10\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=10$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=50,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-10P\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+10Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=50$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10P=50\\,⟹\\,P=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "833e99d9-6b45-57ce-aab8-d29682230a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348790} {:answer/id #uuid "d77ad588-3025-5b6f-a064-1f7d6013c706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348791} {:answer/id #uuid "da4ad5b2-7975-5d11-8759-a418339372c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348792} {:answer/id #uuid "0525068b-fa7a-5590-b611-f6b8b1b7cdf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348793} {:answer/id #uuid "00f7245a-fb37-5e11-9b92-bdf761f219c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da4ad5b2-7975-5d11-8759-a418339372c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348792} :answer-field/id #uuid "acbfaca6-677d-5ef2-b894-66bc2465cfb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2024967-e7f4-594b-aec9-b467d4face3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130356" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=24.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+144 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-144 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 12\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-Q=1\\,⟹\\,Q=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=24,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-12P\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x+12Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=24$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12P=24\\,⟹\\,P=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91f13275-a02b-5f86-90cb-58f588ac4f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348797} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9d1bb2-e570-53d2-99f0-ed05c13d9161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348798} {:answer/id #uuid "77bc1b4e-293f-5b09-8c48-c11e6e916fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348799} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca5e9a2-a6cf-55bd-9bf8-3711dc2f22d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348800} {:answer/id #uuid "319d1ba7-d90a-5da0-a1a9-a960d39a7fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77bc1b4e-293f-5b09-8c48-c11e6e916fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348799} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb82f2ab-3883-5597-b8fa-9a49546e49b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18571d5d-c4ec-5ea3-a208-2c81f6135b54" :question/math-academy-id "q-130370" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+65y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+65y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{e}^{4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x)+{e}^{4x}(-7P\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x+7Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n4P+7Q=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=2$ gives $Q=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51d20a33-66f7-51cc-be82-4a87c9e391eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348804} {:answer/id #uuid "71545513-3c59-5eae-b70e-a143a3666aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348805} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7b2a3e-73db-5278-a6df-ee43b48f0357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348806} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbcc6be-0301-57ad-b5c3-721baaa53da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348807} {:answer/id #uuid "837b078a-9ede-5773-928b-285f4e62a805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb7b2a3e-73db-5278-a6df-ee43b48f0357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348806} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ca1596-b14f-546d-926e-91dff972b85a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95540bc4-4505-5474-9fd4-676a2775e174" :question/math-academy-id "q-130371" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+26y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+26y=0,\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-4.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+{e}^{x}(-5P\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+5Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\nP+5Q=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=-4$ gives $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac07a54-a9c9-5637-8baf-719d28ab675c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}-6x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348811} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3a53d5-3690-526f-a59f-fd0938ae5284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}+2x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348812} {:answer/id #uuid "576d069d-49f0-56e7-861a-12283eda78c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348813} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb5167b-a064-5454-88a8-5903f02ce98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}-5x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348814} {:answer/id #uuid "3f78acf3-1f5a-500c-a7d8-aec67db137c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}-x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fb5167b-a064-5454-88a8-5903f02ce98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}-5x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348814} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18ae508-fe95-5dd8-bcff-ec66264912e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d60fdc16-a98e-5a63-a96a-f3e1aec7f662" :question/math-academy-id "q-65116" :question/problem "Given that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ solve the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, any linear combination of the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$  and ${y}_{2}(x)$  must be a solution to the original differential equation.\n\nHence, the general solution is: \n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{e}^{4x}+{c}_{2}x{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nNow, we find constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}){e}^{4x}+4{c}_{2}x{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=-1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1=4(1)+{c}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-5x{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9695b201-3ea6-5309-879a-caf031732b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3e^{-3x} - 6e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151099} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4e31dd-6ad4-543b-bcab-b26c6a17ced1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 7e^{-3x} - 10e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151100} {:answer/id #uuid "00a553cf-b153-5ec4-a802-b73f8922ab27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-10e^{-3x} + 7e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151101} {:answer/id #uuid "02886e96-bf85-5930-b7fa-116bc64c9bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-9e^{-3x} + 6e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151102} {:answer/id #uuid "73b66be1-6e46-53b0-9f89-534c0ab22f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-12e^{-3x} + 9e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00a553cf-b153-5ec4-a802-b73f8922ab27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-10e^{-3x} + 7e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151101} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c1c0b2e-1224-5711-8b65-104884615486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de2c9bcd-a587-5660-97a8-aa4955c427cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-7788" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=0,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-3.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A-4B=2.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-3 \\\\ -3A-4B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-10,B=7.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afb816d8-375d-5e07-8df5-9b832dec3d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-2x+2}-2{e}^{7x-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348818} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7c6f07-551e-566b-94fa-1eb031a524d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x+2}+{e}^{7x-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348819} {:answer/id #uuid "2d95825b-c7f7-5fd4-948d-cf5bc1679bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-3x}-2{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348820} {:answer/id #uuid "46815c9f-11c7-50a1-9914-766d161bbd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{-2x+2}-3{e}^{7x-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348821} {:answer/id #uuid "99abce99-e974-5477-8ba2-058827f4a9c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{3x-3}+{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7c6f07-551e-566b-94fa-1eb031a524d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x+2}+{e}^{7x-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348819} :answer-field/id #uuid "a868008f-bd31-5131-8f06-1fe899a38725" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20ad2806-b368-58f5-b9aa-01f5f872cbb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-7790" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}-14y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-14{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =7.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+7B{e}^{7x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7}=2 \\\\ -2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A={e}^{2},B={e}^{-7}.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}+{e}^{-7}\\cdot {e}^{7x} \\\\ y & ={e}^{-2x+2}+{e}^{7x-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8b20232-be94-5ef5-ab29-08f1f4291271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348825} {:answer/id #uuid "4369179a-51ef-517f-9308-f10f28df6bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7{e}^{4x-4}+9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348826} {:answer/id #uuid "6c30fd98-942c-5e40-9189-abdb37b8c76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{4x-4}-3x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348827} {:answer/id #uuid "63f40c6e-626f-56f4-8291-c9aab2a3674e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{2x-2}-6x{e}^{2x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348828} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1c2f72-02e9-5de1-9f66-f1411b35f143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x-2}-x{e}^{2x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8b20232-be94-5ef5-ab29-08f1f4291271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348825} :answer-field/id #uuid "375549a8-7dbb-544a-9cdc-0ffcf0bb4d5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0ccd8f3-5c55-5b1e-8569-1f7045c0f609" :question/math-academy-id "q-7798" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{4x}+4Bx{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4}=2 \\\\ 4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=11{e}^{-4},B=-9{e}^{-4}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11{e}^{-4}\\cdot {e}^{4x}-9{e}^{-4}\\cdot x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bab2199-ed90-5590-80ea-4dd87b9ae137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348832} {:answer/id #uuid "7978010e-7173-5709-a51f-f967cb2167ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-3x/2+1}-2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348833} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba91760-ff6f-5324-9e84-f0d9c2613758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348834} {:answer/id #uuid "319d4375-f62b-578a-9885-af0f9621bb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{x/2-1}+2x{e}^{x/2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348835} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd7e9ef-71d3-564b-886d-b9aabf10f2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x/2-1}+3x{e}^{x/2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bab2199-ed90-5590-80ea-4dd87b9ae137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348832} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f5db0bb-c444-551a-93b5-46702f40daca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb39f40a-4179-548a-bb21-32e7734a1e56" :question/math-academy-id "q-7815" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\frac{9y}{4}=0,\\,y(1)={e}^{-1/2},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2})(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x/2}+Bx{e}^{-3x/2}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \nSubstituting $y(1)={e}^{-1/2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3/2}+B{e}^{-3/2} & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\ A+B & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2x}+B{e}^{-3/2x}-\\frac{3}{2}Bx{e}^{-3/2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{-3/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}B & =\\frac{1}{2}e \\\\ -3A-B & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=e \\\\ -3A-B=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-e,B=2e.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-e\\cdot {e}^{-3x/2}+2e\\cdot x{e}^{-3x/2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1387b4e-1fbf-5b85-9ba7-839332be7dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348839} {:answer/id #uuid "31950743-40ca-5c07-a953-6b89c041a6c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348840} {:answer/id #uuid "30846dfe-70a0-50b6-a101-e855d313e861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348841} {:answer/id #uuid "302fd1cd-4c94-5aa0-85a7-e80d7e7bc119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348842} {:answer/id #uuid "04fb0f23-43b0-5c60-acce-1ed8d9fafc0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1387b4e-1fbf-5b85-9ba7-839332be7dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348839} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c00e622-277d-513d-8138-f3e2d850ce33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c268af70-f8b6-5cb4-9a8d-a2de421197dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-7892" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{\\pi },\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-P{e}^{\\pi } & ={e}^{\\pi } \\\\ P & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2P{e}^{\\pi }-2Q{e}^{\\pi } & =4{e}^{\\pi } \\\\ -2P-2Q & =4 \\\\ 2-2Q & =4 \\\\ Q & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-1$ and $Q=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecaad093-51b9-5c48-a30f-0e90f3ae7bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{3x}-2{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348846} {:answer/id #uuid "758ff392-ffd7-5e50-961e-e5dfddf9e6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4{e}^{3x}+6{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348847} {:answer/id #uuid "3c370615-6f72-5f33-adc6-745cc9324851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{3x}+{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348848} {:answer/id #uuid "656088ee-de36-5735-af19-9e65fa6f89e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{3x}-{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348849} {:answer/id #uuid "abee8628-593f-5866-a53c-68ea75cbee5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{3x}+4{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecaad093-51b9-5c48-a30f-0e90f3ae7bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{3x}-2{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348846} :answer-field/id #uuid "691143c8-84f3-5e0b-b36c-20da9420f46f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63af8151-cc17-5da4-b2c3-bc920bd1b153" :question/math-academy-id "q-94006" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+15y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3A{e}^{3x}+5B{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n3A+5B=2.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=2 \\\\ 3A+5B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=4,B=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4{e}^{3x}-2{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9db694-9eae-5d89-a77c-fc45ea265da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348853} {:answer/id #uuid "da90fa79-9985-59cd-bb8a-a264870af529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{2x}+{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348854} {:answer/id #uuid "311acd2d-3ce3-50ef-80f2-36936e890c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{2x}+4{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348855} {:answer/id #uuid "44d4828a-1a0b-5637-97d3-b93c7546e189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{2x}+7{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348856} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf53b83-5636-50c4-8088-ce1743a5c480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}+8{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a9db694-9eae-5d89-a77c-fc45ea265da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348853} :answer-field/id #uuid "d93d7db8-e273-593f-8711-5775bcd6bc87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee07bcca-f720-5a99-913c-116159fbe1b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94007" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+8y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+8y=0,\\,y(0)=9,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=24.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=9$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=9.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=24,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2A{e}^{2x}+4B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=24$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A+4B & =24 \\\\ A+2B & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=9 \\\\ A+2B=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=6,B=3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dccb3d31-e17b-505f-9584-a33184329577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-5x}-5{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348860} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7898de-ecd3-530b-9400-22682f3c9011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-5x}-4{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348861} {:answer/id #uuid "45a4a64c-6c3c-5ab6-ab84-6eeb422bfa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348862} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ed11e-e74c-567f-bceb-5706fefe335c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-5x}-2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348863} {:answer/id #uuid "af7bce6a-2ec2-53cf-92c1-7f25e703d97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45a4a64c-6c3c-5ab6-ab84-6eeb422bfa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348862} :answer-field/id #uuid "9526b77f-f046-5720-a9de-df60afc0c69f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd8751d7-ec69-500b-bd36-468efe567de2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94011" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+10y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+10y=0,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-3.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-3,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}-2B{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-3$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-5A-2B=-3.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-3 \\\\ -5A-2B=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=3,B=-6.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d77a79a7-541f-5517-b8f3-7ee6ba0aecaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348867} {:answer/id #uuid "13e0b305-22c9-525c-a023-d2e7884fbf35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348868} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca3eec8-cd11-50a4-8a0f-d5ee06fb1c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348869} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce27735-9b0e-5971-a480-4d617b265903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348870} {:answer/id #uuid "639d7e19-f11f-5797-a731-97c89178a830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)-4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca3eec8-cd11-50a4-8a0f-d5ee06fb1c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348869} :answer-field/id #uuid "95947d8b-a944-568e-a568-3a93e2571e60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8225538-dd4a-5803-9324-e3d8809baf26" :question/math-academy-id "q-94020" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\sqrt{2}P\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+\\sqrt{2}Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}Q=4\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,Q=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "784f32a9-a698-58ec-bd9f-6cb085f1ba7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348874} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ccb4c0-103d-59de-bab3-7c5fe31f32ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348875} {:answer/id #uuid "796c1627-3ead-51de-a4ec-c336e22f4060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348876} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bd9baa-df47-55fa-b5d1-0c517777ae53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348877} {:answer/id #uuid "d4421a39-30f4-5cf5-a99f-5cf2eec56210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "784f32a9-a698-58ec-bd9f-6cb085f1ba7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348874} :answer-field/id #uuid "10447009-d992-5242-a848-1377014b93e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04610593-0b82-57e6-91c1-fedf291e9236" :question/math-academy-id "q-94022" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=0,\\,y(\\pi )=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=7.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-49 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 7\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi )=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-P=-2\\,⟹\\,P=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=7,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7P\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x+7Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=7$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7Q=7\\,⟹\\,Q=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "676dc692-cca3-5b34-82c4-ce656d1969a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348881} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e1c5d8-159a-5b7e-a8b9-082c068c8ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348882} {:answer/id #uuid "0f910652-235b-55d8-ab67-80914cae2701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348883} {:answer/id #uuid "df794363-5215-5e07-aa31-30f157bda7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348884} {:answer/id #uuid "54b45f5e-eedb-50a6-bbbb-137f60cedf89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6e1c5d8-159a-5b7e-a8b9-082c068c8ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348882} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4333e5-200a-5aef-99c0-c269ffae6117" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dce56cba-a1b7-565e-a887-b1008bc35de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94023" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+64y=0,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-64 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 8\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi )=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-8P\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x+8Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8Q=4\\,⟹\\,Q=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "809594a4-bc02-5125-a768-1cd7a1cd76f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348888} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3b9c83-5da5-5871-b9b4-783921d31572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348889} {:answer/id #uuid "bd55a92c-aaa8-5e2b-8071-d42258bad871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-2x}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348890} {:answer/id #uuid "9526f7da-c5a2-5229-8c04-427c82ffec3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348891} {:answer/id #uuid "4c32223b-bd89-5532-8b33-6fee0b6f38ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "809594a4-bc02-5125-a768-1cd7a1cd76f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348888} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb40ba8-5d4d-5a46-bfc6-33ec658a4419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70c4757b-9d7e-52fc-8fec-97d1c574cab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94025" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi },\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{-2x}(-P\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2Q{e}^{-\\pi }-P{e}^{-\\pi } & =6{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -2Q-P & =6 \\\\ -2-P & =6 \\\\ P & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-8$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fd274dd-9694-580c-92a7-2f8bf78ea100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348895} {:answer/id #uuid "0a579546-e4c1-59d0-a83d-670f4b27bba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348896} {:answer/id #uuid "46209e10-053f-5477-9ffb-14d20ee6c367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348897} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6f04e5-9cec-5fa5-8822-3f40cf85586d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348898} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3f42bc-b34d-5d0a-907c-4add7f007a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f6f04e5-9cec-5fa5-8822-3f40cf85586d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348898} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba24e72d-d7a4-5de5-8b92-92810ffdb068" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dcf262e7-0d66-5dd9-af09-8f0d163d72ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-94030" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+13y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+13y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{-3x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3P+2Q=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=2$ gives $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$"}]}
